    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      Wire Mesh for Concrete Reinforcement: Specs, Placement, and Standards
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 11 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Welded wire mesh is a valid concrete reinforcement for slabs-on-ground, pavement, and shotcrete when crack control—not structural load—is the primary requirement. The most common specs are 4 mm wire at 100 × 100 mm openings (light residential/commercial slabs) and 6 mm wire at 200 × 200 mm openings (industrial slabs), supplied to ASTM A974 or ASTM A185. Place the mesh in the upper third of the slab on chairs that give 40–75 mm of cover, lap sheets by one full opening plus 150 mm minimum, and tie laps with wire. Mesh is <em>not</em> a substitute for rebar in suspended slabs, beams, columns, or any element designed to carry tensile bending loads—there you need deformed bar per the structural drawing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#mesh-vs-rebar" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Welded Wire Mesh vs Rebar: When Each Is the Right Choice</a></li>
